  CORE ARGUMENT: An edited collection of essays reassessing Engels's intellectual legacy after the fall of Soviet communism, when the question of whether Marxism's failures were traceable to Engels's formulations became particularly urgent. Contributors examine Engels's formulation of historical materialism, his philosophy of science, and his relationship to the subsequent tradition of "scientific Marxism."

  RELEVANCE: The volume directly addresses the project's central questions: did Engels's systematization distort Marx's philosophy of history? Essays cover the base-superstructure metaphor, the late letters on determinism, and the Engelsian concept of "scientific socialism."

  POSITION: Pluralistic reassessment of Engels; generally sympathetic to the Engels-divergence thesis.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-legacy, historical-materialism, scientific-socialism, Carver, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{carver1983marxengels,
  author = {Carver, Terrell},
  title = {Marx and Engels: The Intellectual Relationship},
  year = {1983},
  publisher = {Indiana University Press},
  address = {Bloomington},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carver argues that Marx and Engels were substantially different
  thinkers whose intellectual relationship has been systematically misrepresented.
  Rather than a unified "Marxism," Carver traces specific divergences in method,
  philosophy of science, and historical theory, arguing Engels brought a positivist
  systematizing tendency alien to Marx's more open-ended dialectical approach.

  RELEVANCE: This book established the modern Anglophone anti-Engelsian tradition
  and set the terms of the subsequent "Marx-Engels problem" debate. For the broader
  review of Engels's philosophy of history, Carver's argument that Engels
  misrepresented the "materialist conception of history" as a finished system is
  directly relevant to evaluating Engels's own historical-theoretical texts.

  POSITION: Foundational anti-Engelsian divergence thesis; disagrees with the
  "two Marxisms are essentially one" position defended by Hunley and Rigby.
  },
  keywords = {Marx-Engels-problem, Carver, divergence, anti-Engelsian, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{hunley1991lifeengels,
  author = {Hunley, J. D.},
  title = {Life and Thought of Friedrich Engels},
  year = {1991},
  publisher = {Yale University Press},
  address = {New Haven},
  doi = {10.2307/j.ctt211qx5f},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hunley offers a comprehensive intellectual biography that
  defends Engels against anti-Engelsian critics. He argues that alleged
  divergences from Marx are overstated, that Engels's naturalistic and
  dialectical commitments were shared by Marx himself, and that Engels's
  independent theoretical contributions (especially in Anti-Duhring and
  Dialectics of Nature) are largely continuous with the joint project.

  RELEVANCE: As a systematic revisionist defense, Hunley provides the
  primary counter-thesis to Carver and Levine. For this review's assessment
  of Engels's philosophy of history, Hunley's argument that Engels did not
  distort historical materialism is a necessary counterweight to anti-Engelsian
  readings.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense of Engels's fidelity to Marx; pairs with
  Rigby 1992 as the principal scholarly rebuttal to the divergence thesis.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-biography, revisionist-defense, historical-materialism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{rigby1992engelsformation,
  author = {Rigby, S. H.},
  title = {Engels and the Formation of Marxism: History, Dialectics and Revolution},
  year = {1992},
  publisher = {Manchester University Press},
  address = {Manchester},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Rigby examines Engels's contribution to Marxism's genesis
  before and after 1848, asking how far Engels departed from an earlier shared
  paradigm and to what extent Marx himself shared Engels's intellectual
  trajectory in later years. He argues that many core issues of Marxist
  thought -- the debt to Hegelianism, the nature of historical materialism,
  the relationship between class and gender -- were addressed most explicitly
  by Engels, but that this reflects division of labor rather than distortion.

  RELEVANCE: Rigby provides the most thorough analytical treatment of
  Engels's formation of Marxism as a scholarly question, covering key texts
  (Anti-Duhring, Origin of the Family, Ludwig Feuerbach) that are central
  to the review's scope. His argument that Engels's historical materialism
  is continuous with Marx's directly contests Levine's divergence thesis.

  POSITION: Revisionist defense from historical rather than philosophical
  angle; treats the Marx-Engels problem as empirically resolvable rather
  than a matter of philosophical interpretation.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-formation-marxism, revisionist, historical-materialism, Rigby, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{levine2006divergentpaths,
  author = {Levine, Norman},
  title = {Divergent Paths},
  year = {2006},
  publisher = {Lexington Books},
  address = {Lanham},
  doi = {10.5771/9780739154304},
  abstract = {Divergent Paths is the first volume of a groundbreaking three-volume work. Its purpose is to explore the relationship between Hegel and Marx; to define the relationship between Hegel and Engels; and to distinguish between the theories of Marxism and Engelsism. Marx used Feuerbach towards the critique and ultimate transformation of Hegel's phenomenology and humanism. This transformation, which cut out Hegel's idealism by identifying the environment in which people produced their sustenance as the subject of history, marks the genesis of historical materialism. Marx continued to use Hegel's logical categories. In chapter three of Divergent Paths, Norman Levine conducts an in depth study of Marx's 1841 doctoral dissertation, The Difference Between Democritus' and Epicurus' Philosophy of Nature. It is the center of gravity and controversy of Levine's study. Placed alongside Hegel's Philosophy of History, Levine isolates the categories Marx appropriated from Hegel to show, conclusively, that Marx was not a dialectical materialist. Levine then claims that Engels totally distorted the Hegelian legacy, and this debasement is enshrined in his 1887 essay 'Ludwig Feuerbach and The End of Classical German Philosophy.' Levine brilliantly locates Marxism as the theory of Marx, and Engelsism the theory of Engels. According to Levine both embodied a separate view of history and society, and their contradictions are expressive, in part, of their divergent receptions of Hegel.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Levine argues that Hegel was a foundational but fundamentally
  different resource for Marx and Engels, and that Engels totally distorted the
  Hegelian legacy -- especially in his 1888 "Ludwig Feuerbach and the End of
  Classical German Philosophy." Levine distinguishes "Marxism" (grounded in
  Hegelian method and human praxis) from "Engelsism" (which transposes dialectics
  onto nature and treats historical laws as deterministic), arguing these represent
  genuinely divergent philosophical traditions.

  RELEVANCE: This is the most systematic statement of the anti-Engelsian
  divergence thesis with specific focus on the Hegelian dimension. For the
  review's treatment of Engels's philosophy of history, Levine's argument
  that Engels's deterministic laws of history misread both Hegel and Marx
  is a central interpretive challenge that any account must address.

  POSITION: Leading anti-Engelsian position; extends Levine's 1975 "Tragic
  Deception" thesis with more developed textual argumentation.
  },
  keywords = {Levine, anti-Engelsian, Hegel, Engelsism, divergence-thesis, High}
}

@book{carver2014politicalhistory,
  author = {Carver, Terrell and Blank, Daniel},
  title = {A Political History of the Editions of Marx and {E}ngels's {``}{G}erman {I}deology {M}anuscripts{''}},
  year = {2014},
  publisher = {Palgrave Macmillan US},
  address = {New York},
  doi = {10.1057/9781137471161},
  abstract = {Since the 1920s, scholars have promoted a set of manuscripts, long abandoned by Marx and Engels, to canonical status in book form as The German Ideology, and in particular its 'first chapter,' known as the 'Feuerbach chapter.' This book is a detailed account of the editorial and political history behind successive editions.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carver and Blank trace the editorial history of the German
  Ideology manuscripts from their first reconstruction in the early Soviet
  period through successive editions, arguing that what is received as a
  canonical jointly-authored text is in fact an editorial construction that
  imposed a coherence the manuscripts lack. Different political contexts
  shaped which manuscripts were selected, combined, and presented as
  authoritative statements of historical materialism.

  RELEVANCE: The editorial history of German Ideology is central to evaluating
  Engels's role in shaping Marx's legacy and the joint-authorship debate.
  This book provides the most detailed primary-source treatment of how
  "Marx and Engels" as a textual unity was manufactured, directly relevant
  to assessing Engels's contribution to historical materialism.

  POSITION: Textual-editorial approach that sidesteps the divergence/fidelity
  framing in favor of historicizing the manuscripts themselves.
  },
  keywords = {German-ideology, editorial-history, MEGA, Carver, Blank, High}
}

@article{carver2010germanideology,
  author = {Carver, Terrell},
  title = {The {G}erman {I}deology Never Took Place},
  journal = {History of Political Thought},
  year = {2010},
  volume = {31},
  number = {1},
  pages = {107--127},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carver argues that the German Ideology as conventionally
  understood -- a unified founding text of historical materialism -- never
  existed as such. The opening chapter "I. Feuerbach" was factitiously
  constructed by Soviet editors to solve the problem posed by Marx's 1859
  autobiographical reference to "self-clarification." Engels's subsequent
  labeling of this as the "materialist interpretation of history" further
  shaped how the manuscripts were received, but this framing is textually
  untenable.

  RELEVANCE: Directly challenges the traditional reading of German Ideology
  as evidence of a unified Marx-Engels historical theory. For any account of
  Engels's philosophy of history, Carver's textual deconstruction raises
  fundamental questions about which texts can legitimately be attributed to
  the collaboration.

  POSITION: Radical anti-Engelsian position that denies the foundational
  status of the German Ideology as a joint text.
  },
  keywords = {German-ideology, Carver, textual-history, joint-authorship, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{carver2019whosehand,
  author = {Carver, Terrell},
  title = {Whose Hand is the Last Hand? {T}he New {MEGA} Edition of ``{T}he {G}erman {I}deology''},
  journal = {New Political Science},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {41},
  number = {1},
  pages = {140--148},
  doi = {10.1080/07393148.2019.1559131},
  abstract = {There never was a book. "The German Ideology" never was an authorial title. The manuscript "chapter-beginning" for "I. Feuerbach" is barely two pages long. The content of the posthumously published...},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carver uses the 2017 MEGA2 critical edition to revisit
  questions of handwriting attribution in the German Ideology manuscripts,
  arguing that the new edition -- while presenting the manuscripts
  non-linearly and without artificial chapter divisions -- still leaves
