  RELEVANCE: As the most comprehensive collected reassessment of Engels
  encompassing the full range of "Marx-Engels problem" interpretations, this
  volume is an essential reference for the synthesis section. It documents
  the state of the debate at the end of the twentieth century and reveals
  the extent of interdisciplinary engagement with the Marx-Engels relationship.

  POSITION: Pluralistic collection representing multiple interpretive positions;
  includes both anti-Engelsian and revisionist contributions.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-after-Marx, collected-essays, intellectual-legacy, bicentenary, Low, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{byron2022engelsianism,
  author = {Byron, Chris},
  title = {Engelsianism, Second Internationalism, and the Loss of {Marx}'s Critical Method},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2022},
  volume = {50},
  number = {4},
  pages = {535--556},
  doi = {10.1080/03017605.2023.2199584},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that the dominant understanding of "Marxism" as a system comprising Dialectical Materialism and Historical Materialism was produced by Engels and consolidated by Second International thinkers including Lenin and Trotsky. Marx himself never used either phrase and, Byron argues, explicitly rejected the content of these theories. Engels's writings on Capital provide the origin-point for both doctrines, and the Second International's limited access to Marx's own texts meant they depended on Engels's proclamations as their guide to "Marxism."

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the domain's central question — Engels as the creator of "Marxism" as a worldview — and provides a textual case for the divergence between Marx's critical method and the systematic philosophy Engels constructed. Important for assessing how Engels's philosophy of history was transmitted and transformed.

  POSITION: Represents the strong "Engels problem" thesis: Engels distorted Marx and the Second International perpetuated the distortion.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-Marx-divergence, Second-International, dialectical-materialism, orthodoxy, High},
  abstract = {This essay argues that the predominant view of ‘Marxism’ was largely shaped by Engels and the Second International. The predominant view of Marxism includes a metaphysical theory (Dialectical Materialism) and a Scientific Theory (Historical Materialism). Marx never used either phrase. Moreover, a close reading of his mature works and letters reveals that he explicitly rejected the content of those theories. Hence, in the shaping of ‘Marxism’, a series of confusions and inaccuracies about history and economic development were attributed to Marx. One source of miscommunication was Engels, who seems to have misunderstood Marx’s theories in Capital. Moreover, Engels’ writings provide the origin point for ‘Dialectical’ and ‘Historical Materialism’. These attributions to Marx were taken up by Lenin and Trotsky in the Second International. Unlike Engels, Lenin and the Second International had access to few of Marx’s writings, so they had to rely on Engels’ proclamations on Marx’s work for their understanding of ‘Marxism’. Subsequently, ‘Marxism’ has been perverted, as it relates to Marx’s actual method of analysis and presentation, in his critical engagement with capitalism. In this essay the author charts the miscommunication of Marx’s theories, and then provides the reader with Marx’s actual critical method for analyzing Capitalism.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{gronow2016formation,
  author = {Gronow, Jukka},
  title = {On the Formation of {Marxism}: {Karl Kautsky}'s Theory of Capitalism, the {Marxism} of the {Second International} and {Karl Marx}'s Critique of Political Economy},
  year = {2016},
  publisher = {BRILL},
  doi = {10.1163/9789004306653},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Provides a systematic reconstruction of Kautsky's economic and political theory in relation to Marx's Critique of Political Economy, arguing that while Kautsky's political practice suffered from persistent weaknesses, his theory of capitalism — especially before 1910 — was more sophisticated than the standard caricature of mechanical determinism allows. Distinguishes between "Kautsky's Marxism" and "Marx's Marxism" as two different theoretical projects.

  RELEVANCE: The most detailed recent scholarly treatment of Kautsky's theoretical formation; establishes the intellectual content of the "orthodox Marxism" that Engels's texts helped constitute, and provides the detailed basis for evaluating Engels's influence on Second International theory.

  POSITION: Partial rehabilitation of Kautsky's theoretical sophistication while acknowledging the political limitations of his determinism.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Second-International, formation-of-Marxism, capitalism-theory, High},
  abstract = {Preface and Acknowledgements Introduction PART I: KAUTSKY'S MARXISM 1. Organised Capitalism, the General Cartel and the Proletariat 2. The Dispute over Revisionism 3. The Theory of Immiseration, Socialist Consciousness and the Intellectuals 4. Socialism as Science 5. The Capitalist Law of Appropriation: Kautsky's Interpretation of Karl Marx's Economic Thought 6. The Centralisation of Capital and Monopoly Formation 7. Imperialism and the Relation between Industrial and Agrarian Countries 8. Imperialism and Its Alternatives 9. Imperialism as the Last Stage of Capitalism 10. Theoretical Sources of Kautsky's and Lenin's Studies on Imperialism 11. Imperialism as the Truth about Capitalism 12. Parliamentary Democracy and Revolutionary Tactics 13. The Question of Democracy and Dictatorship: Lenin's Critique of Kautsky the Renegade PART II: MARX'S MARXISM 14. The Immanent Critique and the Natural Rights Theory 15. John Locke, Adam Smith and Karl Marx's Critique of Private Property 16. The Principle of Labour 17. The Theory of Increasing Misery and the Critique of Capitalism Conclusion Bibliography Index},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{blackledge2006kautsky,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Karl {Kautsky} and {Marxist} Historiography},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {70},
  number = {3},
  pages = {337--359},
  doi = {10.1521/siso.70.3.337},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Kautsky's historical writings — on the early Church, the Reformation, and contemporary social formations — to argue that his theory of history, especially before 1910, was sophisticated and powerful, not the mechanical economic determinism critics have assumed. The political weaknesses in Kautsky's Marxism are separated from its theoretical merits.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the evidential basis for distinguishing between Kautsky's actual theoretical positions and the "orthodox Marxist" label applied retrospectively; relevant to assessing how much of the Second International's "fatalism" can be traced to Engels's texts versus subsequent political pressures.

  POSITION: Kautsky rehabilitation camp; treats Second International Marxism as more theoretically nuanced than commonly supposed.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Marxist-historiography, Second-International, rehabilitation, High},
  abstract = {The traditional dismissive characterization of Karl Kautsky's Marxism as a form of mechanical or even Darwinian evolutionism is a caricature that obscures the very real and important contribution he made to historical materialism in the early years of the last century. Kautsky's historical studies of both the early Church and Reformation “communism,” as well as his attempts to analyze the contemporary Russian and American social formations, show that, while Kautsky's Marxism suffered from consistent political weaknesses, his theory of history, especially as it was articulated in the years before 1910, was sophisticated and powerful. While the power of Kautsky's Marxism became muted after this period, the weaknesses of his later works should not be allowed to obscure the insights contained within his earlier work; insights that informed some of the best work of the next generation of Marxists.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{nygaard2009constructing,
  author = {Nygaard, Bertel},
  title = {Constructing {Marxism}: {Karl Kautsky} and the {French Revolution}},
  journal = {History of European Ideas},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {35},
  number = {4},
  pages = {450--464},
  doi = {10.1016/j.histeuroideas.2009.04.002},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Shows that Kautsky's writings on the French Revolution were crucial not only to constructing a Marxist interpretation of that event (which dominated 20th-century historiography) but to constructing "Marxism" itself as a comprehensive systematic theory with historical studies as its evidential base. Kautsky is seen as actively building Marxism as a Weltanschauung through historical work.

  RELEVANCE: Illuminates the process by which "Marxism" was constituted as a worldview through the theoretical labor of Second International figures — the same process in which Engels's texts (especially Anti-Dühring) were used as the methodological foundation. Directly connects to the question of how Engelsian categories shaped the practice of Marxist historiography.

  POSITION: Treats Kautsky as a constructive (rather than merely derivative) theorist of orthodox Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Marxism-construction, French-Revolution, Weltanschauung, High},
  abstract = {Karl Kautsky's writings on the French Revolution were crucial to the construction not only of the Marxist interpretation of the Revolution, which was perhaps the most important reference point for the historiography of that event during the 20th century, but even of Marxism itself as a comprehensive, systematic theory partly based on historical studies. However, these writings have been neglected and practically forgotten for decades, mainly because of the general rejection of Kautsky's theories after the October Revolution of 1917, in Marxist as well as non-Marxist circles. Studying these writings, spanning roughly four decades from 1889 till 1930, we may see dynamic interrelations between historical study, theory construction and contemporary political intervention. Kautsky's approach to such key Marxist concepts as class and state prove to be much more subtle and nuanced than what has commonly been assumed, incorporating the results of historical study rather than pure social theory. Yet, his account does contain important internal tensions and contradictions between agency and objective conditions and between the historical material and the normative and political perspective of the historiographer. Several of these internal tensions were carried on into mainstream Marxist accounts of the Revolution, with important historiographical as well as political consequences.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{lih2018kautsky,
  author = {Lih, Lars T.},
  title = {The tasks of our times: {Kautsky}'s {Road to Power} in {Germany} and {Russia}},
  journal = {Studies in East European Thought},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {70},
  number = {2-3},
  pages = {121--140},
  doi = {10.1007/s11212-018-9308-2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Analyzes the reception of Kautsky's Road to Power (1909) in Germany and Russia, arguing that the text was received in fundamentally different ways: as an attack on reformism in Germany, and as a template for revolutionary strategy in Russia. Shows how the same text functioned differently in distinct political contexts, complicating simple accounts of "orthodox Kautskyism."

  RELEVANCE: The differential reception of a canonical Second International text shows how Engelsian and Kautskyan orthodoxy was appropriated and transformed in different national contexts, directly relevant to tracing the line from Engels through Kautsky to Bolshevism.

  POSITION: Revisionist account that complicates the standard narrative of Kautsky as a passive determinist.
  },
  keywords = {Kautsky, Road-to-Power, Second-International, Russia, reception, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{levine2007corruption,
  author = {Levine, Norman},
  title = {Corruption and Fate of Left-Wing {Hegelianism}},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2007},
  volume = {35},
  number = {1},
  pages = {79--102},
  doi = {10.1080/03017600701238489},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Traces the "corruption" of the Left-Hegelian philosophical tradition through Engels's systematization of Marx's thought into a quasi-naturalistic dialectics, arguing that Engels imposed a Feuerbachian materialism onto what had been a more properly Hegelian method in Marx's mature work. This corruption passed through Plekhanov and Kautsky into Soviet diamat.

  RELEVANCE: One of the clearest formulations of the philosophical genealogy from Engels through the Second International to Soviet Marxism, with attention to the Left-Hegelian context. Directly relevant to assessing Engels's philosophical departures from Marx and their long-term consequences.

  POSITION: Strong "Engels problem" position: Engels's systematization corrupted the original Hegelian-critical impulse in Marx.
  },
  keywords = {Hegelianism, Engels-distortion, Plekhanov, diamat-genealogy, High},
  abstract = {Friedrich Engels introduced two fundamental distortions to Karl Marx's original theory. Engels was the inventor of dialectical materialism. Influenced by the development of 19th-century natural science, his close friend Karl Schorlemmer was a chemist, handicapped by a sophomoric understanding of Hegelian philosophy, Engels fused dialectic and the physical sciences. Dialectical materialism, an Engelsian invention, maintained that nature, and history, functioned in terms of dialectical principles. Second, under the Stalinist system this Engelsian distortion became the philosophic foundation, Diamat, of Soviet Marxism. The dialectical materialism of Engels was turned into a metaphysics and was used as an instrument to validate the dictatorship of Stalin. The original message of Marx was maimed by this Engelsian intervention and only the collapse of the Soviet Union opened the opportunity to rethink Marx's original position and to cleanse Marxism from this Engelsian debasement.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{shaw1988plekhanov,
  author = {Shaw, William H.},
  title = {Plekhanov on the role of the individual in history},
  journal = {Studies in Soviet Thought},
  year = {1988},
  volume = {35},
  number = {3},
  pages = {247--265},
  doi = {10.1007/bf01044986},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Plekhanov's account of the individual in history — particularly his claim in The Role of the Individual in History (1898) that great individuals are the product of their times rather than its cause — and analyzes the tension between this determinism and Plekhanov's own active political role. Discusses how Plekhanov attempted to reconcile socialist determinism with political agency.

  RELEVANCE: Plekhanov's treatment of history is the direct link between Engels's philosophy of history (especially its naturalistic-determinist strain) and Russian/Soviet Marxism. Shaw's analysis clarifies the specific doctrinal content of that link and its tensions with political practice.

  POSITION: Analytical reconstruction; identifies both the strengths and internal tensions of Plekhanov's determinist philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, role-of-individual, determinism, diamat-genealogy, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{oizerman2017dialectical,
  author = {Oizerman, Teodor I.},
  title = {On a Critical Reflection on Dialectical Materialism},
  journal = {Russian Studies in Philosophy},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {55},
  number = {2},
  pages = {98--121},
  doi = {10.1080/10611967.2017.1313653},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: A critical internal assessment of dialectical materialism by a leading Russian philosopher, arguing that while Engels and Plekhanov provided the foundation for the doctrine, its subsequent Soviet codification under Stalin involved significant philosophical impoverishment. Oizerman distinguishes the genuine philosophical content in Engels and Plekhanov from the dogmatic schema that became "diamat" in the Stalin period.

  RELEVANCE: Provides an authoritative internal assessment of the Engels-Plekhanov-Lenin-Stalin line of philosophical development, identifying where genuine philosophical work ends and doctrinal codification begins. Important for understanding what was at stake in the creation of Soviet diamat.

  POSITION: Nuanced internal critique: distinguishes genuine philosophical contributions in the Engels-Plekhanov tradition from later dogmatic codification.
  },
  keywords = {dialectical-materialism, Engels, Plekhanov, Soviet-philosophy, Stalin, Medium},
  abstract = {This article offers an analysis of dialectical materialism. The author, being a supporter of this theory, offers a self-critical assessment of its foundations. He argues that the predecessors of Marxism constructed their systems with the confidence that they were building the true and only true philosophy. This utopian idea shared by Marx, Engels, and their successors was refuted by subsequent developments in philosophy. Indeed, philosophy by its very nature is pluralistic and interminable. Self-critical Marxism must recognize the legitimacy not only of dialectical materialism, but also of other philosophies no matter how much they may differ in their content. This is supported by analysis of the main categories and problems of dialectical materialism—the laws of dialectics, matter, evolution, consciousness, and truth.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{sockol2023plekhanov,
  author = {Sockol, David},
  title = {Verso of the {``Vulgar''} Portrait: {Georgi Plekhanov}'s Anti-{Kantianism} and Activist Aesthetic},
  journal = {Rethinking Marxism},
  year = {2023},
  volume = {35},
  number = {4},
  pages = {484--501},
  doi = {10.1080/08935696.2023.2251333},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Challenges the standard portrayal of Plekhanov as a crude "vulgar Marxist" by examining his anti-Kantian philosophical polemics and his aesthetics. Argues that Plekhanov's work, while indebted to Engels's naturalistic materialism, contains a more nuanced aesthetic theory and philosophical program than the "vulgar Marxism" label suggests.

  RELEVANCE: Relevant to understanding the complexity of the Engelsian legacy in Plekhanov: the same move that labels Plekhanov "vulgar" often functions to contain criticism of Engels. Sockol's rehabilitation parallels the Kautsky-rehabilitation literature and raises similar questions about retrospective distortion.

  POSITION: Revisionist; challenges the "vulgar Marxism" framework as applied to Plekhanov.
  },
  keywords = {Plekhanov, vulgar-Marxism, aesthetics, anti-Kantianism, rehabilitation, Medium},
  abstract = {This essay examines Georgi Plekhanov, founder of the Russian Marxist movement and a supposed exemplar of “vulgar Marxism.” It analyses Plekhanov's formulation of a Marxist aesthetic, interrogating the widespread image of Plekhanov's “vulgar” focus on economic determinism and his neglect of cultural forces' influence on revolutionary praxis, locating him instead within a fuller history of Marxist thought. A contextualization of Plekhanov's fin-de-siecle writings on art demonstrates that these texts were effectively a component of his contemporary polemics against critics of Marxist theory, and that within them he was pursuing his polemical agenda of criticizing Kantian philosophy and defending Marxist materialism. These dual aims manifested in Plekhanov's rejection of the Kantian notion of aesthetic disinterestedness and a stress on the formative role class conflict exerted on artistic creation, ultimately resulting in his formulation of an original aesthetic theory prescribing political commitment and thus mobilizing artworks towards revolutionary ends.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{carver2017making,
  author = {Carver, Terrell},
  title = {Making {Marx} {Marx}},
  journal = {Journal of Classical Sociology},
  year = {2017},
  volume = {17},
  number = {1},
  pages = {10--27},
  doi = {10.1177/1468795x17691388},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Argues that "Marx" as a canonical figure — the author of a unified body of doctrine — was substantially constructed by Engels through his editorial and popularizing work after Marx's death. The "Marx" that readers and political movements encountered was largely Engels's creation: his selection, framing, and systematization of texts determined what counted as "Marxist."

  RELEVANCE: Provides the most direct formulation of Engels's role in creating the "Marxism" that the Second International received and developed. Directly relevant to the question of Engels's responsibility for orthodoxy, since it locates that responsibility in the textual politics of canon-formation.

  POSITION: Strong constructivist thesis: "Marxism" as doctrine is primarily Engels's construction rather than a reflection of Marx's actual positions.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-Marx-construction, canon-formation, Marxism-origins, High},
  abstract = {The context through which readers have come to know Marx as ‘Marx’ was not discovered. It was constructed by Marx in the first instance, and by others during his lifetime and afterwards. What he told us about himself at different stages of his life has been assimilated in various ways to reinforce what more-or-less authorised biographers think we should know about his ‘life and thought’. These accounts are based on what bibliographers present as his ‘collected works’, graded by significance. Intellectual biographies are couched in a genre that is neither the contingent everyday (since readers are presented with a life story that has ended) nor the publicity-minded autobiographical (as Marx’s self-characterisations certainly were). Marx himself posted a public notice in 1847, putting a project in self-publicity underway. His own autobiographical and auto-bibliographical Preface of 1859, and Engels’ summarising review of Marx’s book of the same year, both had somewhat wider contemporary audiences. In recounting his activities and works, Marx’s inclusions and emphases are quite different from twentieth- and twenty-first-century canons. A Collected Essays project for Marx was mooted by himself in the early 1850s, but the plan was to recirculate only those items which would raise issues that were still politically current. In 1872, a number of German socialists consciously embarked on a political process of constructing Marx and Engels as iconic founding fathers. Their mass recirculation of The Communist Manifesto sparked an enormous number of reprints and translations. This highly readable text made Marx ‘great’. After his death in 1883, the situation changed dramatically. Engels’ republication of Marx’s works with new introductions and prefaces, along with Engels’ own works, promoted Engels’ projects and ideas as following directly from, and intentionally supplementary to, Marx’s ‘thought’. Franz Mehring’s 1902 catalogue listing of the Marx–Engels archival legacy laid the basis for the first biography of Marx, making him a man of ‘great works’. After a false start in 1911–1913, a collected works project was undertaken in the early 1920s by D.B. Riazanov, resulting in 11 volumes of the Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe, discontinued at the outset of World War II. The revivified Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe project of the 1970s to date differs somewhat from Riazanov’s plan and methodology, but the overall outlines are self-consciously similar: works (including some manuscript and discarded ‘works’), ‘economic’ works, correspondence, notebooks and marginalia. These editorial presumptions make Marx and Engels ‘major’ and their interlocutors ‘minor’ and create hierarchies of genre and content. Uniform typographical presentation makes all writings look the same on the page or screen, and with indexing it becomes easy to read Marx as a ‘thinker’ delivering items of ‘thought’. Thus, editorial practices have worked to mystify the ‘everyday Marx’ and to erase vital and productive ways to think about politics, activism and struggle.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{blackledge2019politics,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {{Engels}'s Politics: Strategy and Tactics after 1848},
  journal = {Socialism and Democracy},
  year = {2019},
  volume = {33},
  number = {2},
  pages = {23--45},
  doi = {10.1080/08854300.2019.1644579},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines Engels's political strategy and tactics after 1848, arguing against the reduction of his thought to mechanical determinism or fatalism. Blackledge shows that Engels combined a long-term historical perspective on capitalism's development with concrete attention to political opportunity and socialist organization, and that his relationship to the Second International was more critical and nuanced than often assumed.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses whether Engels was responsible for the fatalism and passivity associated with Second International orthodox Marxism, by examining his actual political writings and strategic arguments. Important for distinguishing Engels's own positions from the uses to which his successors put them.

  POSITION: Defends Engels against the charge of mechanical determinism while acknowledging tensions in his political thought.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-politics, strategy, fatalism, Second-International, Medium},
  abstract = {While Friedrich Engels’s The Condition of the English Working Class is almost universally praised as an honest, insightful and indeed profound picture of working-class life in Manchester in the 1840s, much of the rest of his contribution to Marxism has typically been dismissed as popularization that reduced Marx’s sublation of materialism and idealism to a warmed-over version of eighteenth-century materialism. What is more, it is this “Engelsian”Marxism that is said to have informed the mechanical and authoritarian interpretations of Marxist politics that became hegemonic on the twentieth-century left. Criticisms of Engels’s politics have a long pedigree. At the end of the nineteenth century, Eduard Bernstein famously argued that though Engels had in practice embraced modern, constructive, evolutionary and electoral politics he failed to theorize this break with the older destructive and insurrectionary form he and Marx had embraced in the 1840s. Bernstein imagined his own “revisionism” as an attempt to draw the logical theoretical conclusions from Engels’s mature political practice to update socialist theory to modern conditions. Though Bernstein’s critique of Marxism cannot bear critical scrutiny, something like his crude contrast between constructive and destructive or evolutionary and insurrectionary politics has tended to frame even some of the best twentieth-century academic interpretations},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{smith2009antiutopianism,
  author = {Smith, Paul B.},
  title = {Reflections on Aspects of {Marxist} Anti-Utopianism},
  journal = {Critique},
  year = {2009},
  volume = {37},
  number = {1},
  pages = {99--120},
  doi = {10.1080/03017600802598260},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Examines the anti-utopian strand in Marxist thought — the insistence that socialism is "scientific" rather than utopian — tracing it from Marx and Engels through Kautsky and Bernstein to later Marxism. Argues that the Marxist critique of utopianism, while philosophically motivated, carried costs in terms of political imagination and created a fatalism about history that undermined activist politics.

  RELEVANCE: Connects Engels's Socialism: Utopian and Scientific (his most widely-read popularizing text) to the broader pattern of Second International determinism, showing how the "scientific socialism" framing contributed to the fatalist tendency that critics associate with orthodox Marxism.

  POSITION: Critical of the anti-utopian strand; argues that the scientific/utopian dichotomy in Engels and Kautsky had damaging political consequences.
  },
  keywords = {scientific-socialism, anti-utopianism, Kautsky, Bernstein, Engels-popularization, Low},
  abstract = {This article argues that Marx and Engels’ anti-utopianism was consistent and based on objective political and economic criteria. Marx and Engels were critical of the ideas and practices of followers of the early founders of socialist theory. Politically, they argued these followers were sectarian. Theoretically, a utopian understanding of political economy was partial and pseudo-scientific. This explained how the means sectarians promoted for achieving the socialist goal were ineffective. Utopianism consisted of a mismatch between ends and means. It was a form of unrealisable socialism. Marx and Engels did not try to prevent people thinking imaginatively about the classless society of the future. This is a distortion. It originated with Bernstein's revisionism. Stalinism and bourgeois sociology consolidated this misunderstanding. As a result anti-utopian Marxism has appeared to be an inconsistent form of conservative subjectivity.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@book{lukacs1971history,
  author = {Luk{\'a}cs, Georg},
  title = {History and Class Consciousness: Studies in Marxist Dialectics},
  year = {1971},
  publisher = {MIT Press},
  address = {Cambridge, MA},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lukacs argues that Engels illegitimately extended Marx's dialectical method from the historical-social realm to the realm of nature, thereby replacing Marx's subject-object dialectic (in which knowing and being are reciprocally transformed through praxis) with a contemplative reflection theory that treats the knowing subject as passive mirror of an external reality. The essay "What is Orthodox Marxism?" insists that method, not specific doctrines, defines Marxism, and the method requires the mediation of subject and object that a dialectics of nature forecloses. Reification analysis follows from this: capitalist society produces the illusion of a nature-like objectivity in social relations.

  RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of the Western Marxist critique of Engels' Dialectics of Nature and Anti-Dühring; virtually every subsequent debate about Engels' relationship to Marx refers back to it. For the project's domain on Engels' philosophy of history, it establishes the dominant critical framework that scholars have had to engage, challenge, or refine.

  POSITION: Anti-Engelsian Western Marxism; Hegelian-humanist Marxism opposed to Engels' naturalism.
  },
  keywords = {Lukacs, Western-Marxism, dialectics-of-nature, reification, anti-Engelsian, High, INCOMPLETE}
}

@misc{korsch1970marxism,
  author = {Korsch, Karl},
  title = {Marxism and Philosophy},
  year = {1970},
  howpublished = {Monthly Review Press, New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Korsch's 1923 essay (republished 1970) argues that the philosophical dimension of Marxism -- the materialist dialectic as a critical theory of bourgeois society -- was suppressed in the orthodox Marxism of the Second International, which treated Marxism as a positive science rather than a revolutionary critique. Korsch, alongside Lukacs, is one of the first to articulate the Western Marxist case that Engels' systematization turned Marx's revolutionary philosophy into a vulgar materialist worldview. He calls for a recovery of Marx's Hegelian inheritance.

  RELEVANCE: Korsch and Lukacs are regularly paired as the founders of Western Marxism's anti-Engelsian turn. The project on Engels' philosophy of history needs this text to understand why the Western Marxist tradition treated Engels as a philosophical problem rather than a resource.

  POSITION: Anti-Engelsian Western Marxism; Hegelian-historicist reading of Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {Korsch, Western-Marxism, Hegelian-Marxism, anti-Engelsian, philosophy-of-marxism, High, INCOMPLETE}
}

@misc{anderson1976considerations,
  author = {Anderson, Perry},
  title = {Considerations on Western Marxism},
  year = {1976},
  howpublished = {New Left Books, London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Anderson provides a systematic account of the tradition of Western Marxism from Lukacs and Korsch to Sartre and Althusser, arguing that it is defined by a structural divorce between socialist theory and working-class practice, a shift from political economy and strategy toward philosophy and aesthetics, and -- crucially -- an abandonment of the natural sciences and Engels' project of a dialectics of nature. Anderson diagnoses Western Marxism as the product of a series of revolutionary defeats in Europe that drove theory into academicism.

  RELEVANCE: Anderson's account of the "anti-Engelsian" formation of Western Marxism is the single most important secondary source for understanding why Engels was marginalized or critiqued in 20th-century Marxist theory. It frames the entire reception history that the project needs to situate.

  POSITION: Critical historiography of Western Marxism from a Trotskyist-inflected perspective; rehabilitates attention to Engels, political economy, and natural science.
  },
  keywords = {Anderson, Western-Marxism, Engels-reception, political-economy, High, INCOMPLETE}
}

@misc{althusser2005formarx,
  author = {Althusser, Louis},
  title = {For Marx},
  year = {2005},
  howpublished = {Verso, London. Originally published 1965, trans. Ben Brewster},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Althusser argues for a fundamental "epistemological break" in Marx's work (around 1845) that separates the "humanist" young Marx from the "scientific" mature Marx. This break enables Althusser to reread Engels as sharing the scientific Marx's project -- the "theoretical practice" of historical materialism as a science of social formations -- while distancing that project from Hegelian humanism. Althusser's concepts of overdetermination and structural causality implicitly rehabilitate Engels' anti-Hegelian materialism while rearticulating it in structuralist terms.

  RELEVANCE: Althusser's reading of Marx-Engels generates a specific position on Engels: he is not a contaminator of Marx but a co-founder of the science. This counters the Western Marxist anti-Engelsian reading and reframes debates about historical materialism that are central to the project.

  POSITION: Structuralist Marxism; epistemological break thesis; partial rehabilitation of Engels as co-founder of Marxist science.
  },
  keywords = {Althusser, epistemological-break, structuralism, Engels-reception, High, INCOMPLETE}
}

@misc{althusser2016reading,
  author = {Althusser, Louis and Balibar, {\'E}tienne},
  title = {Reading Capital},
  year = {2016},
  howpublished = {Verso, London. Originally published 1965, full trans. Ben Brewster and David Fernbach},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reading Capital develops Althusser's method of "symptomatic reading" and the concepts of "structural causality" and "overdetermination" to reconstruct historical materialism as a theoretical science. Engels appears primarily as Marx's collaborator and first interpreter; Althusser uses Engels' account of the relationship between economy and superstructure to elaborate the concept of "determination in the last instance" while criticizing economic determinism. The text situates historical materialism as a science of modes of production, not a philosophy of history in Engels' teleological sense.

  RELEVANCE: Reading Capital, alongside For Marx, constitutes Althusser's full intervention in the Marx-Engels debate. Its structural approach directly challenges both the Lukacs-Korsch humanist reading and the orthodox Engelsian dialectical materialism, generating a third position relevant to the project's investigation of Engels' philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Structuralist Marxism; recasts the problem of historical materialism away from both Engels' naturalism and Western Marxism's subjectivism.
  },
  keywords = {Althusser, Balibar, structural-causality, historical-materialism, Marx-Engels, Medium, INCOMPLETE}
}

@misc{lenin1909materialism,
  author = {Lenin, Vladimir Il\cprime{}ich},
  title = {Materialism and Empirio-Criticism},
  year = {1909},
  howpublished = {Progress Publishers, Moscow. Various English editions},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Lenin defends Engels' philosophical materialism -- particularly the reflection theory of knowledge from Anti-Dühring and Ludwig Feuerbach -- against the Machist revisionism of Bogdanov, Bazarov, and others within Russian Social Democracy. Lenin treats Engels as the authoritative interpreter of Marxist epistemology: matter is objective reality independent of consciousness, knowledge is a reflection of that reality, and idealism is a form of fideism. He deploys Engels' ontological monism, his critique of Kant's thing-in-itself, and his defense of natural science as the empirical basis for materialism.

  RELEVANCE: Lenin's text is the principal vehicle through which Engels' epistemology was canonized in Soviet Marxism and institutionalized as "dialectical materialism." For the project on Engels' philosophy of history, it shows how Engels' scattered epistemological remarks were systematized into an authoritative doctrine, one that later Western Marxists (Lukacs, Althusser) had to negotiate or reject.

  POSITION: Orthodox Marxist-Leninist appropriation of Engels; foundation of "diamat" as official Soviet philosophy.
  },
  keywords = {Lenin, reflection-theory, dialectical-materialism, Engels-reception, empirio-criticism, High, INCOMPLETE}
}

@article{carling1993analytical,
  author = {Carling, Alan},
  title = {Analytical Marxism and Historical Materialism: The Debate on Social Evolution},
  journal = {Science \& Society: A Journal of Marxist Thought and Analysis},
  year = {1993},
  volume = {57},
  number = {1},
  pages = {31--65},
  doi = {10.1177/003682379305700103},
  abstract = {Darwinian evolutionary theory marries a genetic account of the origin of species to a selectionist account of their subsequent fate. The Marxian theory of history is analogous: class struggle provides an account of the origins of new regimes of production and selection pressures explain their subsequent history. The theory is technologically determinist, but there are three distinct doctrines ascribing primacy in different ways to the technological forces of production over the social relations of production. Natural Primacy is probably true but of limited relevance to the debate about historical process. Intentional Primacy, as advocated by G. A. Cohen, is untenable. Competitive Primacy is a genuinely evolutionary doctrine that may even be true.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Carling surveys the debate within analytical Marxism about Cohen's technological determinist reading of historical materialism and proposes a typology of three primacy theses: Natural Primacy (of forces over relations in a causal sense), Intentional Primacy (Cohen's functionalist version), and Competitive Primacy (an evolutionary-selectionist account). He argues that Intentional Primacy is untenable as Cohen formulates it, while Competitive Primacy offers a genuinely evolutionary reading compatible with Darwinian theory. The article maps the analytical Marxist engagement with the Engelsian legacy in terms of explanatory framework rather than philosophical vocabulary.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a clear map of analytical Marxism's internal debates about historical materialism, showing how Cohen's reconstruction was challenged and modified by other analytical Marxists. Useful for assessing whether analytical Marxism vindicates or dissolves Engels' "scientific" account of history.

  POSITION: Analytical Marxism; critical of Cohen's intentional primacy; proposes evolutionary-selectionist alternative.
  },
  keywords = {Carling, analytical-Marxism, historical-materialism, Cohen, technological-determinism, Medium}
}

@article{tarrit2006brief,
  author = {Tarrit, Fabien},
  title = {A Brief History, Scope, and Peculiarities of {``}Analytical Marxism{``}},
  journal = {Review of Radical Political Economics},
  year = {2006},
  volume = {38},
  number = {4},
  pages = {595--618},
  doi = {10.1177/0486613406293223},
  abstract = {The issue of the article is to describe and evaluate analytical Marxism, a school of thought that occupies a particular position in radical theory. It has presented itself as an attempt to renew Marxism with the tools of non-Marxist philosophy (analytical philosophy), epistemology (logical positivism), and social science (methodological individualism). The main authors are Gerald A. Cohen, who defended historical materialism on the basis of analytical philosophy; John Roemer, who reconstructed Marxian economics with neoclassical tools; and Jon Elster, who interpreted Marxism with methodological individualism. Rational choice theory plays an important role in analytical Marxism. This article deals with the idea that analytical Marxism seems to have turned Marxism into its opposite.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Tarrit offers an intellectual history of analytical Marxism, tracing its formation from Cohen's 1978 book through the September Group to its later dissolution into liberal political philosophy. He argues that analytical Marxism's distinguishing feature is methodological individualism and the rejection of Marxist dialectical method in favor of standard social-scientific explanation. The movement's engagement with Engels' legacy is primarily via Cohen's reconstruction of historical materialism, which it accepts as the proper rendering while dropping the philosophical apparatus.

  RELEVANCE: Provides necessary context for understanding analytical Marxism as a movement and its relationship to the Engelsian legacy -- specifically how it treats Marx's and Engels' shared "scientific" claims while abandoning their dialectical and philosophical framework.

  POSITION: Analytical Marxism; historical and critical survey from outside the tradition.
  },
  keywords = {Tarrit, analytical-Marxism, Cohen, historical-materialism, methodology, Medium}
}

@article{timpanaro1974considerations,
  author = {Timpanaro, Sebastiano},
  title = {Considerations on Materialism},
  journal = {New Left Review},
  year = {1974},
  volume = {1},
  number = {85},
  pages = {3--22},
  doi = {10.64590/srm},
  abstract = {Perhaps the sole characteristic common to all contemporary varieties of Western Marxism is, with very few exceptions, their concern to defend themselves against the accusation of materialism. Gramscian or Togliattian Marxists, Hegelian-Existentialist Marxists, Neo-Positivizing Marxists, Freudian or Structuralist Marxists, despite the profound dissensions which otherwise divide them, are . . .},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Timpanaro defends a "biological materialism" that insists on the irreducibility of natural constraints -- aging, illness, death -- on human existence, and criticizes Western Marxism for dissolving nature into society through various forms of idealism and "practicism." He explicitly defends Engels' materialism against the Western Marxist anti-Engelsian critique (Lukacs, Korsch, Della Volpe, Colletti), arguing that the separation of society from nature that Western Marxism requires is philosophically untenable and politically disabling.

  RELEVANCE: Timpanaro's defense of Engels' naturalism from a non-Soviet, non-positivist standpoint is the most philosophically sophisticated counter to the Western Marxist critique of Engels. It directly addresses the question of whether Engels' extension of materialism to nature is a philosophical error or a necessary component of Marxist theory, central to the project.

  POSITION: Naturalist defense of Engels; materialist critique of Western Marxism's neglect of biology and natural necessity.
  },
  keywords = {Timpanaro, naturalism, materialism, Engels-defense, Western-Marxism, High}
}

@article{fracchia2013philosophical,
  author = {Fracchia, Joseph},
  title = {The Philosophical Leninism and Eastern {`}Western Marxism{'} of {Georg} {Luk\'acs}},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {1},
  pages = {69--93},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-12341282},
  abstract = {This essay centres on the English translation (2000) of Georg Lukacs's Tailism and the Dialectic (written in either 1925 or 1926). Lukacs is generally heralded as a founding theoretician of a 'Western Marxism', in opposition to 'Eastern' Soviet Marxism, and his most impressive and most influential work, History and Class Consciousness (1923), is generally treated as having rehabilitated Marxist concern with questions of subjectivity. It might therefore come as a surprise when Lukacs in Tailism states that the purpose of History and Class Consciousness was to demonstrate 'that the organisation and tactics of Bolshevism are the only possible consequence of Marxism'. In my view, however, this should already be abundantly clear from History and Class Consciousness. For Lukacs's absorption with proletarian subjectivity was motivated by an obsession with what he saw as its immaturity. And he coined the category of 'reification' in order to explain his disappointed expectations, to explain, that is, why the proletariat did not make a 'socialist' revolution in the 'objectively ripe' situation of an 'imperialist war' created by 'moribund capitalism'.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Fracchia challenges the dominant narrative that identifies Lukacs with the anti-Engelsian Western Marxist tradition, arguing instead that Lukacs' philosophical project in History and Class Consciousness is continuous with Leninism rather than opposed to it. He shows that Lukacs' critique of Engels' nature-dialectic was not a straightforward endorsement of Hegelian idealism but was embedded in a Leninist theory of class consciousness and party; and that the "Eastern" context of Hungarian communism shaped Lukacs' positions in ways that disrupt the standard West-versus-East (critical-versus-orthodox) taxonomy.

  RELEVANCE: Directly addresses the key question of the domain: how to characterize Lukacs' relationship to Engels. By complicating the standard "Western Marxism as anti-Engelsian" narrative, Fracchia opens a more nuanced reading of the tradition's engagement with Engels.

  POSITION: Revisionist historiography of Western Marxism; argues Lukacs' Leninism complicates the anti-Engelsian reading.
  },
  keywords = {Fracchia, Lukacs, Leninism, Western-Marxism, Engels-reception, High}
}

@article{burkett2013lukacs,
  author = {Burkett, Paul},
  title = {Luk{\'a}cs on Science: A New Act in the Tragedy},
  journal = {Historical Materialism},
  year = {2013},
  volume = {21},
  number = {3},
  pages = {3--15},
  doi = {10.1163/1569206x-12341313},
  abstract = {The rejection of the 'dialectics of nature' has long been thought of as the most fundamental factor distinguishing Western Marxism from official Soviet-style Marxism. Yet, in Tailism and the Dialectic, Georg Lukacs -- perhaps the most influential figure in Western Marxism -- strongly endorses the existence of an objective dialectic in nature. A close examination of Lukacs's main writings on science shows, however, that he still in effect denied the possibility of applying dialectical method to nature. This paradox is bound up with a dualistic conception of natural and social science with distinctly adverse implications for the development of an ecological Marxism.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Burkett examines Lukacs' evolving position on natural science and the dialectics of nature, arguing that the standard reading of History and Class Consciousness as a blanket rejection of Engels' nature-dialectic is too simple. Lukacs' later writings, especially the Ontology of Social Being, substantially revise his early position by acknowledging natural preconditions for social reproduction and a limited role for natural-scientific knowledge. Burkett reads this as a tragic trajectory: the early critique of Engels' scientism was philosophically rich but politically disabling; the later reconciliation came too late and in too compromised a form.

  RELEVANCE: Provides close textual analysis of Lukacs' actual engagement with the Engels nature-dialectic question across his career, crucial for the project's domain since the Lukacs-Engels relationship is often presented in static terms.

  POSITION: Sympathetic-critical reading of Lukacs; identifies internal tensions and development in his engagement with Engels' naturalism.
  },
  keywords = {Burkett, Lukacs, natural-science, dialectics-of-nature, Historical-Materialism, Medium}
}

@book{colletti1973marxism,
  author = {Colletti, Lucio},
  title = {Marxism and {Hegel}},
  year = {1973},
  publisher = {New Left Books},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Colletti argues from a Kantian-materialist standpoint that both Hegel and Engels made a fundamental philosophical error in identifying dialectical contradiction with real material opposition, thereby confusing logical and ontological categories. For Colletti, genuine dialectical materialism requires a Kantian dualism of subject and object rather than Hegelian identity philosophy. Engels' dialectics of nature is, on this reading, philosophically incoherent because it imports Hegelian identity philosophy into a materialist framework that cannot sustain it.

  RELEVANCE: Colletti's critique of Engels' dialectics from a Kantian-materialist (rather than Hegelian-humanist) standpoint represents a distinct and important strand of Italian Western Marxism's engagement with Engels. It provides a different philosophical grounding for rejecting the dialectics of nature than Lukacs' praxis-based critique.

  POSITION: Italian Western Marxism (Della Volpe school); Kantian-materialist critique of Hegel and Engels' dialectics.
  },
  keywords = {Colletti, Italian-Marxism, Kant, Hegel, dialectics, Engels-critique, Medium, INCOMPLETE}
}

@incollection{kain1986engels,
  author = {Kain, Philip J.},
  title = {Engels and Dialectics},
  booktitle = {Marx' Method, Epistemology, and Humanism},
  series = {Sovietica},
  year = {1986},
  pages = {109--117},
  publisher = {Springer Netherlands},
  doi = {10.1007/978-94-009-4646-0_5},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Kain argues against the dominant Western Marxist view (Lukacs, Korsch, Schmidt) that locates the Marx-Engels difference primarily in Engels' acceptance of a dialectics of nature and a reflection theory of knowledge. Instead, Kain maintains that the more significant differences between Marx and Engels lie in their treatments of historical materialism and method: Engels is more mechanistic and determinist than Marx in his account of the relationship between base and superstructure, and this mechanicism -- not the nature-dialectic -- is the real source of the "orthodox Marxism" problem.

  RELEVANCE: Kain's chapter directly challenges the framing inherited from Lukacs and enshrined in the Western Marxist tradition, shifting the locus of the Marx-Engels difference from epistemology to the theory of history. This is a direct intervention in the project's core question about Engels' philosophy of history.

  POSITION: Critical revision of Western Marxist anti-Engelsian framework; locates Marx-Engels difference in historical theory rather than epistemology.
  },
  keywords = {Kain, Engels, Marx-Engels-difference, historical-materialism, dialectics, Medium, INCOMPLETE}
}

@article{blackledge2018reproduction,
  author = {Blackledge, Paul},
  title = {Frederick {Engels}, Social Reproduction, and the Problem of a Unitary Theory of Women's Oppression},
  journal = {Social Theory and Practice},
  year = {2018},
  volume = {44},
  number = {3},
  pages = {297--321},
  doi = {10.5840/soctheorpract20186439},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Defends Engels's The Origin of the Family, Private Property and
  the State as a productive resource for social-reproduction feminism, arguing
  that, once its empirical limitations (drawn from Morgan's anthropology) are
  recognised, its analytical core -- the historicisation of women's oppression
  as a form of capitalist social reproduction -- remains both salvageable and
  necessary for a strategy oriented towards women's liberation.

  RELEVANCE: Addresses one of the most contested aspects of Engels's historical
  materialism -- its account of gender and the family -- and argues for Engels
  as a founding text of feminist historical materialism rather than a
  paternalistic diversion from it.

  POSITION: Rehabilitative feminist reading, aligned with social-reproduction
  theory.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-feminist, Origin-of-Family, social-reproduction, High},
  abstract = {In this paper I argue that Frederick Engels’s The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State remains a fundamental resource for anyone wanting to understand the oppression of women as a capitalist form. By re-examining the strengths and weaknesses of Engels’s historicisation of women’s oppression through the lens of the debates opened by second wave feminism, I argue that, once properly understood, we can overcome the limitations of Engels’s book to point to the kind of unitary theory of women’s oppression essential to a strategy adequate to the needs of the struggle for women’s liberation.},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@book{carver2022engelsc21,
  editor = {Carver, Terrell and Rapic, Smail},
  title = {Friedrich {Engels} for the 21st Century},
  year = {2022},
  publisher = {Springer International Publishing},
  series = {Marx, Engels, and Marxisms},
  doi = {10.1007/978-3-030-97138-0},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: An edited volume collecting new essays on Engels's relevance
  for 21st-century debates in ecology, feminism, political strategy, and
  philosophy of science. The volume is framed as a counterweight to the
  received dismissal of Engels in Western Marxist and post-Marxist scholarship,
  arguing that Engels deserves renewed critical engagement on the merits.

  RELEVANCE: Directly represents the bicentenary rehabilitation movement in
  collected form, bringing together contributors from multiple traditions to
  assess what, if anything, is philosophically and politically live in Engels.

  POSITION: Pluralist rehabilitation: critical but generally favourable.
  },
  keywords = {Engels-rehabilitation, bicentenary-2020, edited-volume, High},
  abstract = {Carlos Sánchez has dedicated a lot of thought and ink to two questions: (1) Is there such a thing as “Mexican philosophy”? and (2) If there is such a thing, does it matter? Throughout his career, Sánchez has consistently answered the first question affirmatively.[1]In response to the second, Sánchez has shared that this tradition mattersto himfor personal reasons. Mexican philosophy has enriched his life, providing resources not only for deep philosophical speculation but also for coming to grips with his identity as a Mexican American. Yet, with respect to the question of why this tradition should matterto everyoneregardless of their background, he at one point confessed, “There is, of course, a well-developed and highly nuanced answer to the question as to why one should study Mexican philosophy ‘at all’. But I haven’t found it yet” (2019).
  abstract_source = {ndpr},

Mexican Philosophy for the 21st Centurydelivers a well-developed, highly nuanced, and compelling answer to the question of why one should study Mexican philosophy “at all.” Sánchez’s central task is to “normalize” Mexican philosophyoutside Mexico, or to demonstrate to the broader world of academic philosophy (especially in the United States) that Mexican philosophy has an original, rich, and overlooked set of tools for addressing human crises.[2]His wager is that the process of normalization will also bring about concrete results. Thanks to normalization, teaching, writing about, and promoting this subfield will not be a “risky practice”: papers on topics in the subfield will more regularly be included in generalist journals and delivered at non-specialized conferences; the field at large will recognize the significant contributions that this subfield has made to our collective pursuit of wisdom; and we will all have access to new “counterhegemonic philosophical interventions”, as well as new resources for expanding our syllabi (2023, 5-6).

According to Sánchez, an obstacle in the way of normalizing Mexican philosophy doubles as one of its characteristic features: it is “post-Western”, simultaneously rejecting and embracing a set of concepts, themes, and methods that have defined the history of Western philosophy. The label “post-Western” is one that Sánchez cleverly coins to capture the unique position of Mexican philosophy as caughtin betweenits homegrown and foreign philosophical foundations, and reducible to neither. The body of Sánchez’s book elaborates upon Mexican philosophy’s post-Western character by way of an itemized exposition of key concepts, themes, and methods. It is broken up into seven chapter-length analyses ofrelajo,nepantla,zozobra,corazonadas,tik, the “figure of the world,” and “Mexistentialism,” respectively.

Chapter 1 analyzes Jorge Portilla’s concept of “relajo”. In the context of Portilla’s phenomenology,relajorefers to a behavior or attitude that disrupts the collective effort to realize value by displacing an audience’s attention. The meaning of life unfolds as we take value seriously, living up to its demands and sincerely engaging in realizing it.Relajotransforms otherwise meaningful pursuits into totally chaotic events. Sánchez artfully illustrates the concept with real-world scenarios, including the January 6, 2021 attack on the United States Capitol, college lectures, town hall meetings, and run-ins with uptight HOA presidents. When a student interrupts a lecture with a joke, and the joke cascades into a commotion (some might ask what the joker said, others laugh, others scoff, etc.), the seriousness of the lecture has been suspended, andrelajohas entered the building.

Sánchez closes this chapter with a fascinating reflection onrelajo’s decolonial potential. Colonization uproots native values, replaces them with values that uphold colonial rule, and codifies the structures that perpetuate those values, so that everyone in that society takes these new values seriously. In this context,relajomight seem like an anti-colonial, decolonial, or outright liberatory maneuver, severing a community’s ties to colonial values. Sánchez cautions that the very structure ofrelajopreventsit fromfullyliving up to its liberatory potential (2023, 48-51). Although, it can perhaps be atemporarymeans of resistance. Accordingly, Sánchez insists that his efforts in this book may contain elements ofrelajo, but his is a more constructive venture.

Chapter 2 turns tonepantla: a Nahua concept that refers to the state of existing in between two identities, temporalities, or ways of being. Sánchez, echoing Emilio Uranga (see 2021, 167), shows us thatnepantlais one of many “autochthonous” concepts that guide an original philosophical program, tracing the term back from 16th-century Indigenous-Spanish dictionaries through the history of Mexican philosophy to the present. Beyond simply providing a descriptive genealogy of the term, Sánchez concludes this chapter by speculating about the prescriptive power ofnepantla.Nepantlaopens us to the possibility that we are not living embodiments of absolute ideals but creatures who permanently exist in between places, times, states, conditions, identities, and so on. As such, Sánchez suggests, it represents a solid foundation for critiquing American individualism, mass consumption, flawed theories of race and sex, and other existential maladies rooted in a misleadingly static picture of existence.

Chapter 3 tackles one of the trickiest concepts to pin down in the history of Mexican philosophy:zozobraor the feeling that accompanies choosing to existin two contradictory states of being simultaneously. Sánchez provides an array of examples that further illustrate this feeling, including a sustained reflection on life during the peak of the COVID-19 quarantine and the experience of simultaneously lovingandhating someone. Sánchez’s treatment of the concept is comprehensive. He lists numerous characteristics Uranga associates with the term and ties each to relevant passages in a handy table (2023, 82-83). He also animates the difference betweenzozobraand other key concepts from European existentialism, such as anxiety, ambiguity, and nausea. Though this chapter is mostly expositional—defining and clarifying the meaning of this important concept—Sánchez emphasizes thatzozobracan serve a social purpose, establishing a foundation for authentic interpersonal connections (2023, 80) and community (2023, 87).

Relajo,nepantla, andzozobraare better-known concepts from the history of Mexican philosophy. Chapter 4 is the first of several sustained analyses of lesser-known concepts. Acorazonadais a heartfelt certainty—not a mere hunch—that results from a direct, intuited, lived experience of one’s circumstances. Etymologically rooted in the Spanish word for “heart” (“corazón”), the term implies “that the heart, and not the mind, is a privileged source of, or access to, justification, truth, or revelation” (2023, 93). One noteworthy feature of this chapter is its careful, historically informed contextualization of the concept. Sánchez contrastscorazonadaswith Kantian, Husserlian, Bergsonian, and Heideggerian senses of “intuition” (§2) and compares it to mundane and esoteric senses of “gut feeling” (§§3-4).},
}

@book{saito2021engelslegacy,
  editor = {Saito, Kohei},
  title = {Reexamining {Engels}'s Legacy in the 21st Century},
